							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">Peace all,<br /><br />Yeah, it&#039;s taking longer than I expected, but my self-defense dvd is still moving forward. <br /><br />Between the actual drunken violence that goes down at clubs (and outside of clubs) and the reaction of the mayor and police chief, I feel that I need to add my voice to a subject that hip-hop doesn&#039;t like to face unless we are forced to. If you prepare for the worst, it usually doesn&#039;t happen.<br /><br /><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Features:</span><br />* Defensive tactics instruction based upon attacks caught on video, described in police reports, or experienced first hand.<br /><br />*Detailed explainations of how violence occurs, and cues to look for in preventing it or surviving it.<br /><br />*Interviews with artists, security personel, and an attorney.<br /><br />*Highlights of seminars and demonstrations, including a recent appearance at Dope Emporium (at the Georgetown Arts Festival).<br /><br />* All northwest hip-hop soundtrack featuring artists representing all the major camps: Massline, Undercaste, 206 Zulu, Block Teamsters Union, SportnLife, Soul Gorilla, 4BC, and others. I would list all the artists, but I&#039;m trying to keep this brief...!  <br /><br />For those who can&#039;t wait for the dvd, class is every Monday and Wednesday in Fremont, near  the Aurora Bridge. I&#039;m also available for seminars, expert witness testimony in court, and event security.<br /><br />Visit <a href="http&#58;//www.karatemeansemptyhand.com" target="_blank">www.karatemeansemptyhand.com </a> or <a href="http&#58;//myspace.com/karatemeansemptyhand" target="_blank">myspace.com/karatemeansemptyhand </a> for more info.</td>
